# .nik Versioning

Semantic Versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH) — not a 4th build-number segment. Every change
to any file under `.nik/` bumps exactly one segment, per this rule:

- **MAJOR** — a breaking/structural change: retiring or renaming an artifact that
  existing Units/Intents already reference, moving ownership of a mechanism from one
  role to another (e.g. QA → Unit Runner for test-case generation), changing the folder
  structure, changing what an existing file's trigger/contents mean. A project bootstrapped
  under an older MAJOR version may need its existing `aidlc-docs/` content re-read against
  the new rules, not just picked up as-is.
- **MINOR** — new, backward-compatible capability: a new rule file, a new check added
  to an existing stage, a new field on an existing template (e.g. Section 0's Repo
  field). Units built before the bump still work; they just don't benefit from the
  addition.
- **PATCH** — a correction with no behavior change: fixing a stale cross-reference, a
  dangling citation to something that doesn't exist, a wrong file path, a typo in an
  instruction. The *intended* behavior doesn't change, only the text describing it
  becomes accurate again.

**Where this number lives**: this file is the single canonical source — don't hand-edit
a version number anywhere else. `steering/core-workflow.md`'s own header references this
file rather than restating the number. Each project's `PROJECT-RULES.md` records which
version was in use as of Bootstrap/last refresh, so a Unit built under an older version
can be traced back to exactly what the framework looked like when it was built.

**When to bump**: at the end of a working session (or a coherent batch of related
changes), not per individual file edit — look at everything changed since the last bump
and pick the highest-severity category that applies (one MAJOR-worthy change in a batch
of otherwise-MINOR ones still means a MAJOR bump, not an average).

